# Check two proposed PowerPC handler replacements against Book I, which has just become available

VERDICT line first, then the liveness token `CODEX_BOOK1CHECK_20260910`.

## READ ONLY THESE FILES. DO NOT LIST OR SEARCH DIRECTORIES.

```
C:\DocumentNoSnc\CC\GXEMUL\_scratchpad\ppc_uisa_book1_v201.txt
C:\DocumentNoSnc\CC\GXEMUL\_scratchpad\ppc_pem_32and64_2005.txt
```

The first is new: **PowerPC User Instruction Set Architecture, Book I, Version 2.01, September 2003**,
a `pdftotext -layout` extraction. It was obtained because the 2005 PEM names it as authoritative over
itself (`ppc_pem_32and64_2005.txt:302`). **Where the two disagree, Book I governs.**

You do not need to read any source code. Both replacements are inlined below in full.

**Please check these rather than ratifying them** — they were written against the PEM alone, before
Book I was available, and the whole point of this pass is that the governing document has changed.

## SETTLED (established today; cite rather than re-derive)

* Book I `:4125-4137` gives `rlwinm` as `n <- SH; r <- ROTL32((RS)32:63, n); m <- MASK(MB+32, ME+32);
  RA <- r & m`, and `:578` defines `MASK` as *"1s in positions x through y (wrapping if x > y)"*.
* **Book I never states that the upper 32 bits of RA are cleared.** The 2005 PEM does, unconditionally,
  at `:30065`. The clearing language in Book I at `:4170-4190` is a programming note about the
  **low-order** 32 bits, for non-wrapping `(MB, ME)`.
* `X(rldimi)`'s rotate loop is `while (sh-- != 0)`, which leaves `sh == -1` for every SH including 0.

## REPLACEMENT A — `rldimi`

```c
X(rldimi)
{
	uint32_t iw = ic->arg[0];
	int rs = (iw >> 21) & 31, ra = (iw >> 16) & 31;
	int sh = ((iw >> 11) & 31) | ((iw & 2) << 4);
	int mb = ((iw >> 6) & 31) | (iw & 0x20);
	int rc = ic->arg[0] & 1;
	int m, n = sh;
	uint64_t tmp, s = cpu->cd.ppc.gpr[rs];

	while (n-- != 0) {
		int b = (s >> 63) & 1;
		s = (s << 1) | b;
	}
	m = mb; tmp = 0;
	do {
		tmp |= ((uint64_t)1 << (63-m));
		m = (m + 1) & 63;
	} while (m != ((64 - sh) & 63));
	cpu->cd.ppc.gpr[ra] &= ~tmp;
	cpu->cd.ppc.gpr[ra] |= (tmp & s);
	if (rc)
		update_cr0(cpu, cpu->cd.ppc.gpr[ra]);
}
```

## REPLACEMENT B — `rlwimi`

```c
X(rlwimi)
{
	uint32_t tmp = (uint32_t)reg(ic->arg[0]);
	MODE_uint_t ra = reg(ic->arg[1]);
	uint32_t iword = ic->arg[2];
	int sh = (iword >> 11) & 31;
	int mb = (iword >> 6) & 31;
	int me = (iword >> 1) & 31;
	int rc = iword & 1;

	tmp = sh ? ((tmp << sh) | (tmp >> (32-sh))) : tmp;

	/*  A wrapping MASK(MB+32, ME+32) includes the high word.  */
	if (mb > me)
		ra = (ra & 0xffffffffULL) | ((uint64_t)tmp << 32);

	for (;;) {
		uint64_t mask;
		mask = (uint64_t)1 << (31-mb);
		ra &= ~mask;
		ra |= (tmp & mask);
		if (mb == me)
			break;
		mb ++;
		if (mb == 32)
			mb = 0;
	}
	reg(ic->arg[1]) = ra;
	if (rc)
		update_cr0(cpu, ra);
}
```

`MODE_uint_t` is `uint64_t` in the 64-bit compilation and `uint32_t` in the 32-bit one; the file is
compiled twice. `reg(x)` reads a `MODE_uint_t` register.

## QUESTIONS

1. **Quote Book I's `rldimi` entry** with line numbers — its RTL and its prose. Does Book I give the
   mask as `MASK(MB, 63-SH)`, and does it agree with the PEM on the endpoint and the wrapping?
2. **Does replacement A compute exactly that mask, for all 4096 (MB, SH) pairs?** In particular check
   `SH = 0` (where `(64 - sh) & 63` is `0`, so the loop's termination value equals its start when
   `mb == 0`) and the wrapping case `MB > 63-SH`. **Is there any (MB, SH) for which the `do/while`
   fails to terminate, or visits a position twice?**
3. **Quote Book I's `rlwimi` entry** with line numbers. Does it give `rA <- (r & m) | (rA & ¬m)` with
   `m <- MASK(MB+32, ME+32)`, and does Book I say anything about `rA`'s upper half for this
   instruction specifically?
4. **Does replacement B produce Book I's result in all four quadrants** — `{MB <= ME, MB > ME}` x
   `{32-bit compilation, 64-bit compilation}`? I am particularly unsure about two things: whether the
   `if (mb > me)` assignment is correctly placed relative to the loop that then mutates `mb`, and
   whether the 32-bit compilation is genuinely unchanged by it.
5. **Is `update_cr0` fed the right value in each?** Book I says the record form sets CR0 from the
   contents of `rA` after the instruction. Both replacements pass the final destination value — check
   that this is what Book I requires, and say whether the comparison WIDTH is specified by the
   instruction or by the current mode.
6. **What does Book I say about `rlwnm`**, and does replacement B's reasoning transfer to it
   unchanged? I have not proposed a replacement for it.
7. **Name any place where Book I and the 2005 PEM disagree** on these three instructions, beyond the
   upper-half sentence already established. I would rather find the disagreements now than after a
   repair ships.

Answer in full. Do not write any file; return the whole answer and I will save it.

